<!--
    calltargettype (GH-869): a call through a parameter, a global or a field
    of a synthesized record gives that storage itself the function-pointer
    type the call states, so the call compiles without a cast. A cast at the
    call is left for a target whose type is declared otherwise.

    Stage: P5 type recovery, just before casts are decided, and the type
    definitions P9 prints. P0 assertion: option calltargettype on|off.

    Source, gcc 11 -O1 -fno-pic -fcf-protection=none, linked with text at
    0x100000 and gfp at 0x200000:

        struct ops { int tag; int (*add)(int, long); void (*done)(void); };
        int (*gfp)(int, long);
        int viaparam(int (*f)(int, long), int x) { return f(x, 3) + 1; }
        int viaglobal(int x) { return gfp(x, 5) * 2; }
        int viafield(struct ops *o, int x) { o->done(); return o->add(x, o->tag); }

    Every pass runs with structsynth param, which mints struct_0 for viafield's
    record, and structdefs on, which prints it above the body.

    Pass 1 (ctypes on, option calltargettype off, the shipped catalog default)
    is the bug: the parameter and the record's two fields are void *, so each
    call is a call of a void * that no C compiler accepts (asserts 1, 5). Pass 2
    (calltargettype on, what the aggressive preset selects) declares the
    parameter and the fields with the types their calls state (asserts 2, 6,
    7), and every call is still printed bare (asserts 3, 4, 8, 9). The global
    gets the type in the function too; nothing in the body prints it, and no
    cast is added at its call (assert 4).

    Pass 3 declares the record, with add as void * and done as a prototype-less
    code *, the way a declaration that does not say what it holds reaches kuna.
    That type is declared, so done is cast at its call instead (assert 10); the
    local add is read into takes the call's type itself (assert 11).

    Pass 4 (Rust output, calltargettype off then on) spells every pointer to
    code *const (), so the option changes nothing there (assert 12).

    Assertions span the combined output of the four passes.
-->
